## What CO₂ therapy and Maria's Gas are

Natural carbon dioxide (CO₂) of volcanic origin rises from the bedrock around Mariánské Lázně (Marienbad), traditionally known as **Maria's Gas**. It emerges from the Maria Spring in the town centre, has a CO₂ content of 99.7% and is one of the four natural healing resources of Marienbad, alongside the mineral springs, peat and the healing climate.

The spa uses it in three procedures: the dry gas bath, the carbonated mineral bath and gas injections. All three rest on the fact that carbon dioxide passes through the skin and acts above all on the small vessels of the skin and subcutaneous tissue. Which of them a patient receives, and how many, is decided by the spa physician at the initial examination.

{% figure
   src="/images/library/treatments/co2-gas-wrap-relax.jpg"
   alt="Woman resting with eyes closed, a silver dry gas bath bag laid over her abdomen"
   caption="The dry gas wrap is applied to the abdomen — the gas is absorbed through the skin during a short rest."
   width="wide" /%}

The therapeutic use of natural CO₂ in Marienbad goes back to the 19th century, when local physicians began giving carbonated baths; subcutaneous application of the gas developed in the 20th century.

{% treatment-box
   title="Dry CO₂ gas bath"
   description="During a dry gas bath the patient rests in a special bag filled with natural CO₂. The procedure usually lasts 20–30 minutes and its suitability is assessed by a physician."
   icon="gas" /%}

## How the individual procedures work

### 1. Dry CO₂ gas bath (the Marienbad gas wrap)

The patient lies in a sealed bag reaching to the neck or applied to part of the body; the bag is filled with natural gas, which is absorbed through the skin. The procedure usually lasts 20–30 minutes and is taken lying down, at rest. During it most guests notice a sensation of warmth and slight tingling of the skin, a sign that the skin vessels have widened. Nothing special is needed for the procedure — only comfortable clothing and time for a short rest afterwards.

### 2. Carbonated mineral bath

A bath in mineral water in which natural CO₂ bubbles. The water is 33–34 °C, cooler than an ordinary warm bath; thanks to the gas acting on the skin vessels, the patient nevertheless perceives it as warm. The bath usually lasts 20 minutes, and fine bubbles form on the skin during it. It is followed by a rest lying down, which is part of the procedure. A detailed overview of what has been published on carbonated baths can be found in the article [CO₂ baths: what the science says](/en/magazine/co2-baths-science).

{% youtube videoId="7C7nqIF7yZc" title="Carbonated mineral baths in Mariánské Lázně" /%}

