   src="/images/library/mineral-bath/co2-apparatus-tub.jpg"
   alt="A man in a historic tub beside a brass apparatus for the carbonated mineral bath"
   caption="The carbonated bath in a historic spa cabin — the gas bubbles up in the water itself."
   width="wide" /%}

### 3. CO₂ gas injections (insufflation)

A physician or trained nurse uses a fine needle to apply a small amount of natural CO₂ under the skin — most often around the large joints, along the spine or in the lower limbs. The sites and their number are chosen by the physician according to the diagnosis. During the application the patient feels slight pressure or a "bubbling" under the skin, which subsides within moments; the gas is absorbed and the patient can carry on with the day's programme. The procedure is described in detail in the article [CO₂ gas injections](/en/magazine/co2-gas-injections).

{% figure
