   alt="A therapist holds a handheld probe, connected to a device, to a patient's neck and shoulder"
   caption="Insufflation: a small volume of gas is applied under the skin, straight into the painful spot."
   width="wide" /%}

## How long a course lasts

CO₂ procedures are prescribed as a course: as a rule several times a week for the whole stay, often combined with other procedures (peloids, therapeutic exercise, the drinking cure). The number of procedures and their frequency are set by the spa physician, who may adjust them during the stay.

The length of the stay is governed not by the procedure but by the item of the Czech indication list you arrive with. For hypertension of the second to third degree (II/3), for example, the basic stay is 28 days in comprehensive care or 21 days in contributory care; for arterial disease of the limbs (II/4) it is 21 days in both types of care. Self-paying guests choose the length in consultation with the spa physician.

## Which conditions it is used for

CO₂ procedures are not a separate item of the Czech indication list; they are procedures the spa physician builds into the treatment plan for the diagnosis the patient arrives with. In the Czech indication list they are among the basic procedures for diseases of the circulatory system (indication group II):

{% indication-picker group="circulatory" title="Diseases of the circulatory system in the indication list" /%}

On the basis of long spa practice the physician also prescribes them for diseases of the musculoskeletal system (indication group VII) — above all gas injections around painful joints and along the spine — and in the after-care of chronic wounds as a supplement to the care led by the treating doctor. A complete overview of diagnoses is on the [indications and contraindications](/en/indications-and-contraindications) page.

## When CO₂ therapy is not suitable

The general contraindications of spa rehabilitation care under the indication list apply, among others:

- all diseases in the acute stage and conditions in which a deterioration of health can be expected;
- clinical signs of circulatory failure, malignant arrhythmias and persistent hypertension above 120 mm Hg diastolic pressure;
- conditions within 3 months of deep vein thrombosis and within 6 weeks of the resolution of superficial thrombophlebitis;
- recurrent profuse bleeding in the past 12 months;
- infectious diseases transmissible from person to person;
- pregnancy.

Gas injections are additionally not given where there is acute inflammation or a skin disease at the injection site, or in severe blood-clotting disorders. The complete list of contraindications is on the [indications and contraindications](/en/indications-and-contraindications) page.

## Who decides on inclusion

Whether any CO₂ procedure is included in the treatment plan, and of which kind, in what number and at what frequency, is decided by the spa physician after the initial examination; for patients with hypertension or arterial disease, in cooperation with their treating doctor. CO₂ therapy supplements the treatment a patient already has; it does not replace it. If you notice unusual complaints during the course, tell the spa physician — the plan can be adjusted.

## What a patient can realistically expect

A review of CO₂ balneotherapy in cardiovascular disease summarises three measurable effects of the carbonated bath: increased skin blood flow, a slight drop in body temperature and a sensation of warmth ([Pagourelias et al., 2011](https://consensus.app/papers/details/cffd890e39e252b5bf9e66f5a36c8ff6/), *Int J Biometeorol*). What controlled studies have documented beyond that:

- **Skin blood flow and walking in arterial disease of the lower limbs.** In a randomised crossover trial in 18 patients with peripheral arterial disease of the lower limbs, immersing the feet in carbonated water raised laser-measured skin blood flow by 300% and tissue oxygen by 10%; in plain water the flow did not change ([Hartmann et al., 1997](https://doi.org/10.1177/000331979704800406), *Angiology*). In a follow-up randomised trial in 24 patients with claudication, a four-week course of foot baths (five times a week, 30 minutes) increased both tissue oxygen and the distance patients could walk without pain ([Hartmann et al., 1997](https://doi.org/10.1177/000331979704801104), *Angiology*). Both studies used artificially carbonated water and had small samples.
- **Blood pressure.** In a randomised trial in 70 women aged 48–60 with climacteric syndrome, mean daytime systolic pressure fell by 13.7% after ten dry CO₂ baths ([Chekhoeva et al., 2024](https://consensus.app/papers/details/e4851982210856378938913590dccac8/), *Bull Rehabil Med*). The control group received no treatment and this was a specific group of women; the result therefore cannot be transferred to all patients with hypertension.
- **Healing of chronic wounds.** In a double-blind randomised trial of 57 chronic diabetic wounds, 20 of 30 wounds healed after 20 applications of CO₂ gas to the skin, and none in the air-treated control group ([Macura et al., 2020](https://doi.org/10.1111/iwj.13436), *Int Wound J*). This was gas applied to the skin, a method close to the dry gas bath, not a water bath or injections; the sample was small.

For the musculoskeletal system and for gas injections, CO₂ procedures are used on the basis of long spa practice as part of a broader programme drawn up by the spa physician. How the procedures affect you is assessed by the physician during your stay.

